---
import PageLayout from "@/layouts/Base.astro";

const sections = [
	{
		id: "preamble",
		number: "01",
		title: "Preamble",
		subtitle: "Historical context and architectural motivation",
	},
	{
		id: "architecture",
		number: "02",
		title: "Architecture Overview",
		subtitle: "Full forward-pass data flow",
	},
	{
		id: "embeddings",
		number: "03",
		title: "Embeddings",
		subtitle: "Tokenization, embedding matrices, and positional encoding",
	},
	{
		id: "attention",
		number: "04",
		title: "Attention",
		subtitle: "Scaled dot-product attention and QKV projections",
	},
	{
		id: "multi-head-attention",
		number: "05",
		title: "Multi-Head Attention",
		subtitle: "Structured decomposition into parallel subspaces",
	},
	{
		id: "ffn",
		number: "06",
		title: "Feed-Forward Network",
		subtitle: "Position-wise MLP and learned key-value memories",
	},
	{
		id: "residual-stream",
		number: "07",
		title: "Residual Stream",
		subtitle: "Residual connections, running sums, and layer normalization",
	},
	{
		id: "output",
		number: "08",
		title: "Output",
		subtitle: "Unembedding, softmax, and autoregressive sampling",
	},
	{
		id: "circuits",
		number: "09",
		title: "Circuits",
		subtitle: "Cross-layer head composition and mechanistic interpretability",
	},
];
---

<PageLayout meta={{ title: "How Transformers Work" }}>
	{/* Hero — full width, above the three-column layout */}
	<header class="hero mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 pb-16 pt-8 text-center sm:px-6 sm:pt-16">
		<h1 class="mb-6 text-4xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent-2 sm:text-5xl lg:text-6xl">
			How Transformers Work
		</h1>
		<p class="mx-auto max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-textColor/80 sm:text-xl">
			An interactive technical reference to the transformer architecture — from
			tokenization to circuits, with inline citations.
		</p>
		<div class="mt-10 flex items-center justify-center gap-3 text-sm text-textColor/50">
			<span>Toby Lunt</span>
			<span class="text-accent/40">|</span>
			<span>2026</span>
			<span class="text-accent/40">|</span>
			<span>~30 min read</span>
		</div>
		<div class="mx-auto mt-12 h-px w-24 bg-accent/30"></div>
	</header>

	{/* Three-column body: TOC | Content | Sidenotes */}
	<div class="transformers-layout">
		{/* Left TOC sidebar — desktop only */}
		<nav id="toc-sidebar" class="toc-sidebar" aria-label="Table of contents">
			<div class="toc-inner">
				<ul class="toc-list">
					{
						sections.map((section) => (
							<li>
								<a
									href={`#${section.id}`}
									class="toc-dot-link"
									data-section={section.id}
									aria-label={section.title}
								>
									<span class="toc-dot"></span>
									<span class="toc-label">{section.number} {section.title}</span>
								</a>
							</li>
						))
					}
				</ul>
			</div>
		</nav>

		{/* Main content column */}
		<article class="transformers-content">

			{/* ===== Section 01: Preamble ===== */}
			<section id="preamble" class="mb-24 scroll-mt-20">
				<div class="mb-8 flex items-baseline gap-4">
					<span class="font-mono text-sm text-accent/60">01</span>
					<div>
						<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-accent-2 sm:text-3xl">Preamble</h2>
						<p class="mt-1 text-base text-textColor/60">Historical context and architectural motivation</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				{/* Introductory prose */}
				<div class="preamble-prose">
					<p>
						The transformer architecture, introduced by Vaswani et al. in 2017,<span class="sn-marker" data-sn="sn-vaswani">1</span>
						replaced recurrent sequence modeling with a fundamentally different computational
						primitive: <strong>self-attention</strong>. The result has been a near-complete displacement
						of prior architectures across language modeling, machine translation, protein structure
						prediction, image generation, and code synthesis.
					</p>
					<p>
						The core limitation of recurrent models (RNNs, LSTMs, GRUs) was their sequential
						bottleneck. Processing a sequence of length <code>n</code> required <code>O(n)</code>
						serial operations, each compressing the entire history into a fixed-dimensional hidden
						state <code>h ∈ ℝ<sup>d</sup></code>. This imposed both a computational constraint
						(no parallelism across timesteps) and an information-theoretic one: long-range dependencies
						had to survive repeated lossy compressions through the same bottleneck. Bahdanau-style
						attention<span class="sn-marker" data-sn="sn-bahdanau">2</span> partially addressed the
						information constraint by allowing the decoder to attend to all encoder states, but the
						sequential processing requirement remained.
					</p>
					<p>
						The transformer eliminated recurrence entirely. Self-attention computes pairwise interactions
						between all positions in <code>O(1)</code> sequential depth, at the cost of <code>O(n²)</code>
						total computation per layer.<span class="sn-marker" data-sn="sn-quadratic">3</span> Every token
						attends to every other token simultaneously — the full context is available at every layer,
						without compression into a fixed-size state. This architectural choice made deep
						models trainable on long sequences and unlocked massive parallelism on modern hardware.
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* Black box teaser */}
				<div class="preamble-teaser reveal">
					<div class="teaser-box">
						<div class="teaser-label-in">tokens in</div>
						<span class="teaser-arrow">→</span>
						<div class="teaser-blackbox">
							<span class="teaser-q">?</span>
						</div>
						<span class="teaser-arrow">→</span>
						<div class="teaser-label-out">prediction out</div>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="section-prose reveal">
					<p>
						The transformer is a black box: tokens in, a probability distribution over next tokens out.
						The following sections decompose this mapping into its constituent operations —
						tokenization, embedding, self-attention, feed-forward layers, and the residual
						stream that connects them.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			{/* ===== Section 02: Architecture Overview ===== */}
			<section id="architecture" class="mb-24 scroll-mt-20">
				<div class="mb-8 flex items-baseline gap-4">
					<span class="font-mono text-sm text-accent/60">02</span>
					<div>
						<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-accent-2 sm:text-3xl">Architecture Overview</h2>
						<p class="mt-1 text-base text-textColor/60">Full forward-pass data flow</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="arch-prose">
					<p>
						The diagram below shows the full forward pass of a decoder-only transformer.<span class="sn-marker" data-sn="sn-decoder-only">4</span>
						An input sequence is tokenized, embedded, summed with positional encodings, then passed
						through <code>N</code> identical layers — each comprising layer normalization, multi-head
						self-attention, a residual addition, layer normalization, a position-wise feed-forward
						network, and a second residual addition.<span class="sn-marker" data-sn="sn-prenorm">5</span>
						The final representation is projected to vocabulary size via an unembedding matrix and
						normalized with softmax to produce a distribution over next tokens.
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* Interactive block diagram */}
				<div class="arch-diagram-wrap">
					<div class="arch-diagram" id="arch-diagram">
						{/* Output / Softmax */}
						<div class="arch-block arch-output" data-arch-id="output" data-tip="Converts the final vector into a probability distribution over the vocabulary — picking the next token.">
							<span class="arch-block-label">Softmax Output</span>
						</div>

						{/* Unembedding */}
						<div class="arch-block arch-output" data-arch-id="unembed" data-tip="Projects the residual stream vector back to vocabulary size via the unembedding matrix — the reverse of token embedding.">
							<span class="arch-block-label">Unembedding</span>
						</div>

						<div class="arch-arrow">&#8595;</div>

						{/* Layer norm (final) */}
						<div class="arch-block arch-norm" data-arch-id="final-norm" data-tip="A final layer normalization that stabilizes the output before unembedding.">
							<span class="arch-block-label">Layer Norm</span>
						</div>

						<div class="arch-arrow">&#8595;</div>

						{/* Transformer layer (repeated) */}
						<div class="arch-layer-group">
							<div class="arch-layer-bracket">&times;N layers</div>

							<div class="arch-add-label">+ residual</div>
							{/* FFN sub-block */}
							<div class="arch-block arch-ffn" data-arch-id="ffn" data-tip="A two-layer neural network (expand → activate → compress) applied independently to each token. Acts as a learned lookup table.">
								<span class="arch-block-label">Feed-Forward Network</span>
							</div>
							<div class="arch-block arch-norm arch-norm-sm" data-arch-id="norm2" data-tip="Layer normalization before the feed-forward network.">
								<span class="arch-block-label">Layer Norm</span>
							</div>

							<div class="arch-add-label">+ residual</div>
							{/* Attention sub-block */}
							<div class="arch-block arch-attention" data-arch-id="attention" data-tip="Multi-head self-attention: every token computes queries, keys, and values, then attends to every other token to gather context.">
								<span class="arch-block-label">Multi-Head Attention</span>
							</div>
							<div class="arch-block arch-norm arch-norm-sm" data-arch-id="norm1" data-tip="Layer normalization before the attention mechanism.">
								<span class="arch-block-label">Layer Norm</span>
							</div>
						</div>

						<div class="arch-arrow">&#8595;</div>

						{/* Positional encoding */}
						<div class="arch-block arch-embedding" data-arch-id="pos-enc" data-tip="Adds position information (sine/cosine waves) to each embedding, so the model knows word order.">
							<span class="arch-block-label">+ Positional Encoding</span>
						</div>

						{/* Token embedding */}
						<div class="arch-block arch-embedding" data-arch-id="embed" data-tip="Looks up each token in a learned embedding table, converting it from a discrete ID into a dense vector.">
							<span class="arch-block-label">Token Embedding</span>
						</div>

						<div class="arch-arrow">&#8595;</div>

						{/* Input */}
						<div class="arch-input-label">Input tokens</div>
					</div>

					{/* Tooltip */}
					<div class="arch-tooltip" id="arch-tooltip" aria-hidden="true"></div>
				</div>

				<p class="arch-outro">
					The sections below decompose each block: tokenization and embedding (§03),
					scaled dot-product attention (§04), multi-head decomposition (§05),
					position-wise FFN (§06), residual connections and layer normalization (§07),
					and the output projection (§08).
				</p>
			</section>

			{/* ===== Section 03: Embeddings — Tokenization ===== */}
			<section id="embeddings" class="mb-24 scroll-mt-20">
				<div class="mb-8 flex items-baseline gap-4">
					<span class="font-mono text-sm text-accent/60">03</span>
					<div>
						<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-accent-2 sm:text-3xl">Embeddings</h2>
						<p class="mt-1 text-base text-textColor/60">Tokenization, embedding matrices, and positional encoding</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				{/* Linkage callout */}
				<div class="linkage-callout reveal linkage-embedding">
					<span class="linkage-icon">&#8599;</span>
					<span>Corresponds to the <strong>Token Embedding</strong> and <strong>+ Positional Encoding</strong> blocks in the architecture diagram.</span>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose reveal">
					<p>
						Transformers operate on sequences of discrete tokens, not raw text. The standard
						tokenization algorithm is Byte Pair Encoding (BPE),<span class="sn-marker" data-sn="sn-bpe">6</span>
						which iteratively merges the most frequent adjacent byte pairs in the training corpus
						to build a vocabulary of subword units (typically 32k–100k tokens). High-frequency
						words remain intact; rare or novel words decompose into shorter subword pieces, ensuring
						complete coverage of arbitrary input text with a fixed vocabulary.
					</p>
					<p>
						The interactive tokenizer below demonstrates this decomposition:
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* Interactive tokenizer widget */}
				<div class="tokenizer-widget">
					<label class="tokenizer-label" for="tokenizer-input">Type some text:</label>
					<textarea
						id="tokenizer-input"
						class="tokenizer-input"
						rows="2"
						placeholder="The fat cat sat quietly on the mat."
						spellcheck="false"
					></textarea>
					<div class="tokenizer-output-wrap">
						<div class="tokenizer-output-label">
							Tokens <span class="tokenizer-count" id="tokenizer-count"></span>
						</div>
						<div class="tokenizer-output" id="tokenizer-output">
							<span class="tokenizer-placeholder">Type above to see tokens&hellip;</span>
						</div>
					</div>
					<div class="tokenizer-legend">
						<span class="tokenizer-legend-item"><span class="token-chip token-whole-example">&nbsp;</span> Whole word</span>
						<span class="tokenizer-legend-item"><span class="token-chip token-sub-example">&nbsp;</span> Subword piece</span>
						<span class="tokenizer-legend-item"><span class="token-chip token-special-example">&nbsp;</span> Punctuation / special</span>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose reveal">
					<p>
						Each token ID indexes into a learned embedding matrix
						<code>W<sub>E</sub> &isin; &reals;<sup>|V| &times; d</sup></code>, producing a dense
						vector <code>e = W<sub>E</sub>[token_id]</code>. These embeddings are trained end-to-end;
						the model discovers a geometry where tokens used in similar distributional
						contexts<span class="sn-marker" data-sn="sn-distributional">7</span> cluster together
						in the <code>d</code>-dimensional space.
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* Token → Vector transition */}
				<div class="embed-transition">
					<div class="embed-transition-label">Each token becomes a dense vector:</div>
					<div class="embed-transition-rows">
						<div class="embed-transition-row">
							<span class="token-chip token-whole">cat</span>
							<span class="embed-arrow">&rarr;</span>
							<span class="embed-vector">[0.23, &minus;0.41, 0.87, 0.05, &hellip;]</span>
						</div>
						<div class="embed-transition-row">
							<span class="token-chip token-whole">dog</span>
							<span class="embed-arrow">&rarr;</span>
							<span class="embed-vector">[0.21, &minus;0.38, 0.91, 0.02, &hellip;]</span>
						</div>
						<div class="embed-transition-row">
							<span class="token-chip token-whole">king</span>
							<span class="embed-arrow">&rarr;</span>
							<span class="embed-vector">[&minus;0.74, 0.62, &minus;0.15, 0.88, &hellip;]</span>
						</div>
					</div>
					<p class="embed-transition-note">
						<strong>cat</strong> and <strong>dog</strong> occupy nearby regions of embedding space (both animals);
						<strong>king</strong> lies in a distant subspace. Projecting to 2D via PCA or t-SNE
						reveals these semantic clusters:
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* 2D Embedding scatter plot */}
				<div class="embed-scatter-wrap">
					<div class="embed-scatter-header">
						<span class="embed-scatter-title">Word embeddings projected to 2D</span>
						<span class="embed-scatter-subtitle">hover or tap any point to see the word</span>
					</div>
					<svg id="embed-scatter" class="embed-scatter" viewBox="0 0 600 420" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet">
						{/* Grid lines */}
						<line x1="60" y1="380" x2="580" y2="380" stroke="currentColor" stroke-opacity="0.08" stroke-width="1"/>
						<line x1="60" y1="300" x2="580" y2="300" stroke="currentColor" stroke-opacity="0.05" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/>
						<line x1="60" y1="220" x2="580" y2="220" stroke="currentColor" stroke-opacity="0.05" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/>
						<line x1="60" y1="140" x2="580" y2="140" stroke="currentColor" stroke-opacity="0.05" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/>
						<line x1="60" y1="60" x2="580" y2="60" stroke="currentColor" stroke-opacity="0.05" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/>
						<line x1="60" y1="60" x2="60" y2="380" stroke="currentColor" stroke-opacity="0.08" stroke-width="1"/>
						{/* Cluster backgrounds — subtle ovals (pointer-events: none so dots are hoverable) */}
						<ellipse cx="140" cy="310" rx="80" ry="55" fill="hsl(210 70% 55%)" fill-opacity="0.04" stroke="hsl(210 70% 55%)" stroke-opacity="0.08" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 3" pointer-events="none"/>
						<ellipse cx="460" cy="110" rx="85" ry="55" fill="hsl(35 90% 55%)" fill-opacity="0.04" stroke="hsl(35 90% 55%)" stroke-opacity="0.08" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 3" pointer-events="none"/>
						<ellipse cx="150" cy="120" rx="75" ry="55" fill="hsl(350 65% 55%)" fill-opacity="0.04" stroke="hsl(350 65% 55%)" stroke-opacity="0.08" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 3" pointer-events="none"/>
						<ellipse cx="440" cy="310" rx="80" ry="55" fill="hsl(150 55% 45%)" fill-opacity="0.04" stroke="hsl(150 55% 45%)" stroke-opacity="0.08" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 3" pointer-events="none"/>
						<ellipse cx="300" cy="210" rx="70" ry="50" fill="hsl(270 50% 58%)" fill-opacity="0.04" stroke="hsl(270 50% 58%)" stroke-opacity="0.08" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 3" pointer-events="none"/>
						{/* Data points will be rendered by JS for interactivity */}
					</svg>
					<div class="embed-scatter-legend" id="embed-scatter-legend"></div>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose reveal">
					<p>
						The embedding space exhibits geometric structure: semantically related tokens
						occupy nearby regions, and linear directions encode relational properties
						(the classic <code>king − man + woman ≈ queen</code> phenomenon). However,
						the embedding matrix is permutation-equivariant with respect to input position —
						<code>W<sub>E</sub>[token]</code> is identical regardless of where in the sequence
						the token appears. Position information must be injected separately.
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* ===== Positional Encoding subsection ===== */}
				<div class="subsection-divider reveal"></div>

				<h3 class="subsection-title">
					<span class="subsection-label">03b</span>
					Positional Encoding
				</h3>

				{/* Linkage callout */}
				<div class="linkage-callout reveal linkage-embedding">
					<span class="linkage-icon">&#8599;</span>
					<span>Corresponds to the <strong>+ Positional Encoding</strong> block, the second input-stage operation.</span>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose reveal">
					<p>
						Self-attention is permutation-equivariant: the output for each token depends only
						on the set of input vectors, not their ordering. Without positional information,
						permuting the input sequence produces correspondingly permuted outputs — the
						model cannot distinguish word order. The original transformer addresses this by
						additive sinusoidal positional encodings:<span class="sn-marker" data-sn="sn-rope">8</span>
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="posenc-formula">
					<div class="posenc-formula-row">
						<span class="posenc-fn">PE</span><sub>(pos, 2i)</sub> = sin(pos / 10000<sup>2i/d</sup>)
					</div>
					<div class="posenc-formula-row">
						<span class="posenc-fn">PE</span><sub>(pos, 2i+1)</sub> = cos(pos / 10000<sup>2i/d</sup>)
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose reveal">
					<p>
						The wavelengths form a geometric progression from <code>2π</code> to
						<code>2π · 10000</code>, giving each position a unique binary-like
						encoding across dimensions. The low-index dimensions oscillate rapidly
						(fine positional resolution); high-index dimensions change slowly
						(coarse resolution):
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* Interactive position slider + wave visualization */}
				<div class="posenc-interactive">
					<div class="posenc-slider-wrap">
						<label class="posenc-slider-label" for="posenc-slider">
							Position: <span id="posenc-pos-value" class="posenc-pos-value">0</span>
						</label>
						<input type="range" id="posenc-slider" class="posenc-slider" min="0" max="31" value="0" step="1"/>
						<div class="posenc-slider-range">
							<span>0</span>
							<span>31</span>
						</div>
					</div>

					{/* Wave visualization — SVG canvas */}
					<div class="posenc-waves-wrap">
						<div class="posenc-waves-label">Encoding vector at position <span id="posenc-wave-pos">0</span></div>
						<svg id="posenc-waves" class="posenc-waves" viewBox="0 0 600 160" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet">
							{/* Bars rendered by JS */}
							<line x1="30" y1="80" x2="580" y2="80" stroke="currentColor" stroke-opacity="0.1" stroke-width="1"/>
							<text x="12" y="14" fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.2" font-size="8" text-anchor="middle">+1</text>
							<text x="12" y="84" fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.2" font-size="8" text-anchor="middle">0</text>
							<text x="12" y="156" fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.2" font-size="8" text-anchor="middle">-1</text>
						</svg>
					</div>
				</div>

				{/* Heatmap of positional encoding matrix */}
				<div class="posenc-heatmap-wrap">
					<div class="posenc-heatmap-label">Positional encoding matrix (32 positions &times; 32 dimensions)</div>
					<canvas id="posenc-heatmap" class="posenc-heatmap" width="480" height="240"></canvas>
					<div class="posenc-heatmap-axes">
						<span class="posenc-axis-label">Dimension &rarr;</span>
						<span class="posenc-axis-label-y">Position &darr;</span>
					</div>
					<div class="posenc-heatmap-scale">
						<span class="posenc-scale-label">&minus;1</span>
						<div class="posenc-scale-bar"></div>
						<span class="posenc-scale-label">+1</span>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose reveal">
					<p>
						The heatmap shows the full positional encoding matrix. Each row is a unique vector —
						the leftmost columns oscillate at high frequency (capturing fine position),
						while rightmost columns vary slowly. The sinusoidal design also allows the model to
						learn relative position via linear projections, since
						<code>PE(pos+k)</code> can be expressed as a linear function of <code>PE(pos)</code>.
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* LINKAGE: embedding + position = input to attention */}
				<div class="posenc-handoff">
					<div class="posenc-handoff-label">The input to attention:</div>
					<div class="posenc-handoff-row">
						<div class="posenc-handoff-box posenc-handoff-embed">
							<span class="posenc-handoff-icon">E</span>
							<span>Token Embedding</span>
						</div>
						<span class="posenc-handoff-plus">+</span>
						<div class="posenc-handoff-box posenc-handoff-pos">
							<span class="posenc-handoff-icon">P</span>
							<span>Position Encoding</span>
						</div>
						<span class="posenc-handoff-eq">=</span>
						<div class="posenc-handoff-box posenc-handoff-result">
							<span class="posenc-handoff-icon">&rarr;</span>
							<span>Input Vector</span>
						</div>
					</div>
					<p class="posenc-handoff-note">
						The input to the first transformer layer is the elementwise sum
						<code>x = W<sub>E</sub>[token] + PE(pos)</code>.<span class="sn-marker" data-sn="sn-shared-space">9</span>
						This vector encodes both token identity and sequential position in a single
						<code>d</code>-dimensional representation.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			{/* ===== Section 04: Attention — Q, K, V ===== */}
			<section id="attention" class="mb-24 scroll-mt-20">
				<div class="mb-8 flex items-baseline gap-4">
					<span class="font-mono text-sm text-accent/60">04</span>
					<div>
						<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-accent-2 sm:text-3xl">Attention</h2>
						<p class="mt-1 text-base text-textColor/60">Scaled dot-product attention and QKV projections</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				{/* LINKAGE: Receiving combined vectors from Feature #7 */}
				<div class="linkage-callout reveal linkage-attention">
					<span class="linkage-icon">&#8599;</span>
					<span>Corresponds to the <strong>Multi-Head Attention</strong> block. Input: position-augmented token embeddings.</span>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose reveal">
					<p>
						Each input vector knows <em>what</em> a token means and <em>where</em> it sits — but
						so far, tokens are completely independent of each other. The word “bank” has the
						same representation whether it appears in “river bank” or “bank account.”
						Attention is how tokens <strong>talk to each other</strong>, gathering context from the
						rest of the sequence to resolve ambiguities like this.
					</p>
					<p>
						The mechanism works through three learned projections. Each input vector is multiplied
						by three different weight matrices — <strong class="qkv-q-text">W<sub>Q</sub></strong>,
						<strong class="qkv-k-text">W<sub>K</sub></strong>, and
						<strong class="qkv-v-text">W<sub>V</sub></strong> — to produce three new vectors:
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* Q, K, V analogy cards */}
				<div class="qkv-analogy-cards">
					<div class="qkv-card qkv-card-q">
						<div class="qkv-card-letter">Q</div>
						<div class="qkv-card-name">Query</div>
						<div class="qkv-card-desc">Retrieval signal: xW<sub>Q</sub></div>
					</div>
					<div class="qkv-card qkv-card-k">
						<div class="qkv-card-letter">K</div>
						<div class="qkv-card-name">Key</div>
						<div class="qkv-card-desc">Match target: xW<sub>K</sub></div>
					</div>
					<div class="qkv-card qkv-card-v">
						<div class="qkv-card-letter">V</div>
						<div class="qkv-card-name">Value</div>
						<div class="qkv-card-desc">Content payload: xW<sub>V</sub></div>
					</div>
				</div>

								<div class="section-prose reveal">
					<p>
						The <strong class="qkv-q-text">query</strong> encodes what information a token is seeking;
						the <strong class="qkv-k-text">key</strong> encodes what information it offers for matching;
						the <strong class="qkv-v-text">value</strong> encodes the content to be retrieved when a
						match occurs.<span class="sn-marker" data-sn="sn-associative">10</span> Compatibility between
						a query and key is measured by their dot product, and the result gates how much of the
						corresponding value is incorporated into the output.
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* Interactive QKV projection visualization */}
				<div class="qkv-viz-container">
					<div class="qkv-controls">
						<button id="qkv-step-btn" class="qkv-btn qkv-btn-step">
							<svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 14 14" fill="none"><path d="M3 2L11 7L3 12V2Z" fill="currentColor"/></svg>
							Step
						</button>
						<button id="qkv-play-btn" class="qkv-btn qkv-btn-play">
							<svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 14 14" fill="none"><path d="M2 1L12 7L2 13V1Z" fill="currentColor"/><rect x="11" y="1" width="2" height="12" rx="0.5" fill="currentColor"/></svg>
							Play
						</button>
						<button id="qkv-reset-btn" class="qkv-btn qkv-btn-reset">
							<svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 14 14" fill="none"><path d="M2 7A5 5 0 1 1 7 12" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" fill="none"/><path d="M2 10V7H5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" fill="none"/></svg>
							Reset
						</button>
						<span id="qkv-step-label" class="qkv-step-label">Step 0 / 9</span>
					</div>
					<div class="qkv-viz-scroll">
						<svg id="qkv-svg" class="qkv-svg" viewBox="0 0 810 420" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
							<!-- Will be populated by JS -->
						</svg>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose reveal">
					<p>
						Each projection maps from <code>d<sub>model</sub></code> to <code>d<sub>k</sub></code>
						dimensions. For a sequence of <code>n</code> tokens, this produces three matrices:
						<strong class="qkv-q-text">Q</strong>,
						<strong class="qkv-k-text">K</strong>,
						<strong class="qkv-v-text">V</strong> <code>&isin; &reals;<sup>n &times; d<sub>k</sub></sup></code>.
						The next step computes pairwise compatibility scores between all query-key pairs.
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* ===== Feature #9: Dot product attention + softmax ===== */}
				<div class="subsection-divider reveal"></div>
				<h3 class="subsection-title"><span class="subsection-label">04b</span> Dot Product Attention &amp; Softmax</h3>

				<div class="linkage-callout reveal linkage-attention">
					<span class="linkage-icon">&#8599;</span>
					<span>Using <strong class="qkv-q-text">Q</strong> and <strong class="qkv-k-text">K</strong> from 04a to compute scaled dot-product attention scores.</span>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose reveal">
					<p>
						Attention scores are computed via the dot product
						<code>q<sub>i</sub><sup>T</sup> k<sub>j</sub></code>, which measures the alignment
						between token <code>i</code>’s query and token <code>j</code>’s key in the projected space.
						The full attention function:
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* Formula display */}
				<div class="attn-formula">
					<div class="attn-formula-row">
						Attention(<span class="qkv-q-text">Q</span>, <span class="qkv-k-text">K</span>, <span class="qkv-v-text">V</span>) = softmax(<span class="qkv-q-text">Q</span><span class="qkv-k-text">K</span><sup>T</sup> / &radic;<span class="attn-formula-dk">d<sub>k</sub></span>) <span class="qkv-v-text">V</span>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose reveal">
					<p>
						<code>QK<sup>T</sup></code> produces an <code>n × n</code> matrix of raw scores.
						The <code>1/√d<sub>k</sub></code> scaling prevents softmax saturation: without it,
						for large <code>d<sub>k</sub></code> the dot products grow as
						<code>O(√d<sub>k</sub>)</code> in expectation (assuming unit-variance entries),
						pushing softmax into regions with vanishing gradients.<span class="sn-marker" data-sn="sn-scaling">11</span>
						After scaling, softmax normalizes each row to a probability distribution.
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* Interactive dot product + softmax visualization */}
				<div class="attn-pipeline-card">
					<div class="attn-pipeline-header">
						How <span class="qkv-q-text">q<sub>2</sub></span> ("big") attends to each key
					</div>
					<div class="attn-pipeline-table" id="attn-pipeline-table">
						{/* Populated by JS — 3 columns (tokens) × 3 rows (raw, scaled, softmax) */}
					</div>
					<div class="attn-pipeline-temp">
						<label class="attn-temp-label" id="attn-temp-display">Temperature: 1.0</label>
						<input type="range" id="attn-temp-slider" class="attn-temp-slider" min="0.1" max="3.0" step="0.1" value="1.0" />
						<div class="attn-temp-range">
							<span>0.1 (sharp)</span>
							<span>3.0 (smooth)</span>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose reveal">
					<p>
						The temperature parameter (<code>T</code>) controls the entropy of the attention
						distribution.<span class="sn-marker" data-sn="sn-temperature">12</span>
						As <code>T → 0</code>, softmax approaches argmax (hard attention);
						as <code>T → ∞</code>, it approaches a uniform distribution. The output of
						each attention head is the weighted sum of value vectors:
						<code>output<sub>i</sub> = ∑<sub>j</sub> α<sub>ij</sub> v<sub>j</sub></code>,
						a convex combination (since attention weights sum to 1).
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* ===== Feature #10: Attention pattern heatmap ===== */}
				<div class="subsection-divider reveal"></div>
				<h3 class="subsection-title"><span class="subsection-label">04c</span> Attention Pattern Heatmap</h3>

				<div class="linkage-callout reveal linkage-attention">
					<span class="linkage-icon">&#8599;</span>
					<span>Computing attention weights for all query positions yields the full <code>n × n</code> attention matrix.</span>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose reveal">
					<p>
						Row <code>i</code> of the attention matrix is the softmax distribution for query token
						<code>i</code>; column <code>j</code> shows the total attention received by key token
						<code>j</code>. In causal (autoregressive) models, the upper triangle is masked to
						<code>-∞</code> before softmax, preventing tokens from attending to future
						positions.<span class="sn-marker" data-sn="sn-causal">13</span>
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="attn-heatmap-container" id="attn-heatmap">
					<div class="attn-heatmap-title">Attention weights for “The big cat sat quietly”</div>
					<div class="attn-heatmap-subtitle">Each row is one token’s attention distribution across all keys</div>

					<div class="attn-heatmap-wrap">
						<div class="attn-heatmap-axis-query">query</div>
						<div class="attn-heatmap-inner">
							<div class="attn-heatmap-axis-key">key</div>
							<div class="attn-heatmap-grid" id="heatmap-grid"></div>
						</div>
					</div>

					{/* Tooltip */}
					<div class="attn-heatmap-tooltip" id="heatmap-tooltip"></div>

					{/* Color scale legend */}
					<div class="attn-heatmap-legend">
						<span class="attn-heatmap-legend-label">0</span>
						<div class="attn-heatmap-legend-bar"></div>
						<span class="attn-heatmap-legend-label">1</span>
					</div>
					<div class="attn-heatmap-legend-caption">Attention weight (darker = stronger)</div>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose reveal">
					<p>
						The diagonal captures self-attention (identity-preserving); off-diagonal entries
						capture inter-token dependencies — e.g., subject-verb agreement or coreference.
						This matrix is computed independently per attention head; multiple heads operating
						in parallel learn to capture different relational patterns (§05).
					</p>
				</div>

				{/* ===== Feature #11: Value-weighted output animation ===== */}
				<div class="subsection-divider reveal"></div>
				<h3 class="subsection-title"><span class="subsection-label">04d</span> Value-Weighted Output</h3>

				<div class="linkage-callout reveal linkage-attention">
					<span class="linkage-icon">&#8599;</span>
					<span>The attention weights from 04b are multiplied with the <strong class="qkv-v-text">V</strong> matrix to produce the head output.</span>
				</div>

				<div class="section-prose reveal">
					<p>
						For each query position <code>i</code>, the output is the convex combination
						<code>o<sub>i</sub> = ∑<sub>j</sub> α<sub>ij</sub> v<sub>j</sub></code>.
						The attention weights <code>α<sub>ij</sub></code> determine how much of each
						value vector contributes. For "cat" (token 2) with weights 0.21, 0.46, 0.33:
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="val-output-container" id="val-output">
					<div class="val-output-title">Computing output for "cat"</div>

					{/* Step-by-step weighted sum */}
					<div class="val-output-steps" id="val-output-steps">
						{/* Row 1: weight × V for "The" */}
						<div class="val-step-row">
							<div class="val-weight" style="opacity: 0.55;">0.21</div>
							<div class="val-times">&times;</div>
							<div class="val-vector val-vector-v">v<sub>1</sub> "The"</div>
							<div class="val-equals">=</div>
							<div class="val-result" style="opacity: 0.55;">[0.04, -0.09, 0.18, ...]</div>
						</div>
						{/* Row 2: weight × V for "cat" */}
						<div class="val-step-row">
							<div class="val-weight" style="opacity: 0.88;">0.46</div>
							<div class="val-times">&times;</div>
							<div class="val-vector val-vector-v">v<sub>2</sub> "cat"</div>
							<div class="val-equals">=</div>
							<div class="val-result" style="opacity: 0.88;">[0.11, 0.37, -0.05, ...]</div>
						</div>
						{/* Row 3: weight × V for "sat" */}
						<div class="val-step-row">
							<div class="val-weight" style="opacity: 0.72;">0.33</div>
							<div class="val-times">&times;</div>
							<div class="val-vector val-vector-v">v<sub>3</sub> "sat"</div>
							<div class="val-equals">=</div>
							<div class="val-result" style="opacity: 0.72;">[0.08, 0.13, 0.22, ...]</div>
						</div>

						{/* Sum line */}
						<div class="val-sum-divider"></div>
						<div class="val-step-row val-step-sum">
							<div class="val-sum-label">output<sub>2</sub></div>
							<div class="val-equals">=</div>
							<div class="val-result val-result-sum">[0.23, 0.41, 0.35, ...]</div>
						</div>
					</div>

					{/* Bar visualization showing weight contribution */}
					<div class="val-bars-container">
						<div class="val-bars-title">Contribution to "cat" output</div>
						<div class="val-bars">
							<div class="val-bar-row">
								<div class="val-bar-label">"The"</div>
