		{/* Right sidenote gutter — desktop only, content added by future features */}
		<aside class="sidenote-gutter" aria-label="Sidenotes">
			{/* Section 01: Preamble sidenotes */}
			<div class="sidenote" id="sn-vaswani"><span class="sn-number">1</span><span class="sn-content">Vaswani, A. et al. “Attention Is All You Need.” <em>NeurIPS</em> 2017. The paper that introduced the transformer, demonstrating state-of-the-art translation with no recurrence or convolution. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762" class="sn-link">arXiv</a></span></div>
			<div class="sidenote" id="sn-bahdanau"><span class="sn-number">2</span><span class="sn-content">Bahdanau, D. et al. “Neural Machine Translation by Jointly Learning to Align and Translate.” <em>ICLR</em> 2015. Introduced additive attention for seq2seq models, allowing the decoder to selectively focus on source positions. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1409.0473" class="sn-link">arXiv</a></span></div>
			<div class="sidenote" id="sn-quadratic"><span class="sn-number">3</span><span class="sn-content">The <code>O(n²)</code> cost of self-attention has motivated substantial work on efficient variants: Linformer, Performer, and Flash Attention (Dao et al. 2022) reduce the practical cost while preserving most of the representational capacity. FlashAttention in particular achieves exact attention in sub-quadratic I/O complexity. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.14135" class="sn-link">arXiv</a></span></div>
			{/* Section 02: Architecture Overview sidenotes */}
			<div class="sidenote" id="sn-decoder-only"><span class="sn-number">4</span><span class="sn-content">The original transformer (Vaswani et al.) used an encoder-decoder architecture for translation. Most modern LLMs (GPT, Llama, Claude) use a decoder-only variant, which processes a single sequence autoregressively. The encoder-decoder design persists in some translation and speech models (T5, Whisper).</span></div>
			<div class="sidenote" id="sn-prenorm"><span class="sn-number">5</span><span class="sn-content">Modern transformers use Pre-Norm ordering: layer norm is applied <em>before</em> each sublayer, not after (as in the original paper). This stabilizes training at depth. See Xiong et al. “On Layer Normalization in the Transformer Architecture,” <em>ICML</em> 2020. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.04745" class="sn-link">arXiv</a></span></div>
			{/* Section 03: Embeddings sidenotes */}
			<div class="sidenote" id="sn-bpe"><span class="sn-number">6</span><span class="sn-content">Sennrich, R. et al. “Neural Machine Translation of Rare Words with Subword Units.” <em>ACL</em> 2016. BPE was adapted from data compression to NLP tokenization, enabling open-vocabulary models. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1508.07909" class="sn-link">arXiv</a></span></div>
			<div class="sidenote" id="sn-distributional"><span class="sn-number">7</span><span class="sn-content">The distributional hypothesis: “You shall know a word by the company it keeps” (Firth, 1957). Embedding geometry is a direct consequence of this principle — tokens co-occurring in similar contexts receive similar gradient updates.</span></div>
			<div class="sidenote" id="sn-rope"><span class="sn-number">8</span><span class="sn-content">Modern models rarely use sinusoidal PE. Rotary Position Embeddings (RoPE; Su et al. 2021) encode relative position by rotating Q and K vectors, enabling length generalization. ALiBi (Press et al. 2022) adds a linear bias to attention scores instead. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.09864" class="sn-link">RoPE</a> &middot; <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.12409" class="sn-link">ALiBi</a></span></div>
			<div class="sidenote" id="sn-shared-space"><span class="sn-number">9</span><span class="sn-content">Adding content and position embeddings in the same vector space is a design choice, not a necessity. It means position information can interfere with semantic content. RoPE avoids this by applying rotations only to Q/K, leaving V (and thus content) unmodified.</span></div>
			{/* Section 04: Attention sidenotes */}
			<div class="sidenote" id="sn-associative"><span class="sn-number">10</span><span class="sn-content">This Q/K/V structure can be viewed as a differentiable associative memory: keys are memory addresses, values are stored content, and the query performs a soft lookup. See Ramsauer et al. “Hopfield Networks is All You Need,” <em>ICLR</em> 2021. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2008.02217" class="sn-link">arXiv</a></span></div>
			<div class="sidenote" id="sn-scaling"><span class="sn-number">11</span><span class="sn-content">If <code>q</code> and <code>k</code> are independent with zero mean and unit variance, their dot product has mean 0 and variance <code>d<sub>k</sub></code>. Dividing by <code>√d<sub>k</sub></code> normalizes the variance back to 1, keeping softmax gradients well-behaved.</span></div>
			<div class="sidenote" id="sn-temperature"><span class="sn-number">12</span><span class="sn-content">In standard attention, there is no explicit temperature parameter — the <code>1/√d<sub>k</sub></code> scaling serves a similar role. Temperature is introduced during generation (applied to output logits, not attention scores). We show it here for pedagogical clarity.</span></div>
			<div class="sidenote" id="sn-causal"><span class="sn-number">13</span><span class="sn-content">The causal mask sets <code>score(i,j) = -∞</code> for <code>j > i</code>, making <code>softmax(…) = 0</code> for future positions. This enforces the autoregressive property: token <code>i</code>’s representation depends only on tokens <code>≤ i</code>. Bidirectional models (BERT) omit this mask.</span></div>
			<div class="sidenote" id="sn-kvcache"><span class="sn-number">14</span><span class="sn-content">During autoregressive generation, previously computed K and V vectors can be cached and reused (the KV cache), reducing per-token cost from <code>O(n · d)</code> to <code>O(d)</code> for the projection step. The KV cache is a major engineering consideration for inference efficiency.</span></div>
			{/* Section 05: Multi-Head Attention sidenotes */}
			<div class="sidenote" id="sn-head-taxonomy"><span class="sn-number">15</span><span class="sn-content">Olsson et al. “In-context Learning and Induction Heads,” <em>Transformer Circuits Thread</em>, 2022. Provides a taxonomy of head types and evidence that induction heads drive in-context learning. <a href="https://transformer-circuits.pub/2022/in-context-learning-and-induction-heads/index.html" class="sn-link">link</a></span></div>
			<div class="sidenote" id="sn-head-pruning"><span class="sn-number">16</span><span class="sn-content">Michel et al. “Are Sixteen Heads Really Better than One?” <em>NeurIPS</em> 2019. Shows that many heads can be pruned at test time with minimal performance loss, suggesting substantial redundancy. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.10650" class="sn-link">arXiv</a></span></div>
			<div class="sidenote" id="sn-gqa"><span class="sn-number">17</span><span class="sn-content">Grouped Query Attention (Ainslie et al. 2023) reduces KV cache memory by sharing K/V projections across groups of query heads. Used in Llama 2 70B and later models. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.13245" class="sn-link">arXiv</a></span></div>
			{/* Section 06: FFN sidenotes */}
			<div class="sidenote" id="sn-gelu"><span class="sn-number">18</span><span class="sn-content">GELU (Hendrycks & Gimpel, 2016) approximates <code>x · Φ(x)</code> where Φ is the Gaussian CDF. It provides smoother gradients than ReLU, particularly around zero. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1606.08415" class="sn-link">arXiv</a></span></div>
			<div class="sidenote" id="sn-swiglu"><span class="sn-number">19</span><span class="sn-content">SwiGLU (Shazeer, 2020) gates the FFN with an additional linear projection: <code>SwiGLU(x) = (xW<sub>1</sub>) ⊗ SiLU(xW<sub>gate</sub>)</code>. Used in PaLM, Llama, and most recent LLMs. Requires 3 weight matrices per FFN instead of 2. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.05202" class="sn-link">arXiv</a></span></div>
			<div class="sidenote" id="sn-ffn-memory"><span class="sn-number">20</span><span class="sn-content">Geva et al. “Transformer Feed-Forward Layers Are Key-Value Memories.” <em>EMNLP</em> 2021. Demonstrates that FFN neurons activate on interpretable input patterns and store corresponding output values. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.14913" class="sn-link">arXiv</a></span></div>
			{/* Section 07: Residual Stream sidenotes */}
			<div class="sidenote" id="sn-elhage"><span class="sn-number">21</span><span class="sn-content">Elhage et al. “A Mathematical Framework for Transformer Circuits.” <em>Transformer Circuits Thread</em>, 2021. Formalizes the residual stream as a shared linear workspace and analyzes head composition. <a href="https://transformer-circuits.pub/2021/framework/index.html" class="sn-link">link</a></span></div>
			<div class="sidenote" id="sn-resnet"><span class="sn-number">22</span><span class="sn-content">He et al. “Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition.” <em>CVPR</em> 2016. Introduced residual connections for training very deep networks. The gradient through a residual block has an identity component that prevents vanishing. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.03385" class="sn-link">arXiv</a></span></div>
			<div class="sidenote" id="sn-layernorm"><span class="sn-number">23</span><span class="sn-content">Ba, Kiros & Hinton. “Layer Normalization.” 2016. Unlike batch normalization, LayerNorm normalizes across the feature dimension of a single example, making it suitable for variable-length sequences. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.06450" class="sn-link">arXiv</a></span></div>
			<div class="sidenote" id="sn-rmsnorm"><span class="sn-number">24</span><span class="sn-content">RMSNorm (Zhang & Sennrich, 2019) normalizes by the root mean square only: <code>RMSNorm(x) = x / RMS(x) · γ</code>, omitting the mean-centering step. Used in Llama and other recent architectures for efficiency. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.07467" class="sn-link">arXiv</a></span></div>
			{/* Section 08: Output sidenotes */}
			<div class="sidenote" id="sn-weight-tying"><span class="sn-number">25</span><span class="sn-content">Press & Wolf. “Using the Output Embedding to Improve Language Models.” <em>EACL</em> 2017. Weight tying reduces parameters and can improve performance by forcing the embedding and unembedding to share the same vector space. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1608.05859" class="sn-link">arXiv</a></span></div>
			<div class="sidenote" id="sn-nucleus"><span class="sn-number">26</span><span class="sn-content">Holtzman et al. “The Curious Case of Neural Text Degeneration.” <em>ICLR</em> 2020. Introduced nucleus (top-p) sampling, which truncates the distribution to the smallest set of tokens whose cumulative probability exceeds <code>p</code>. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.09751" class="sn-link">arXiv</a></span></div>
			<div class="sidenote" id="sn-kvcache-gen"><span class="sn-number">27</span><span class="sn-content">The KV cache stores all previously computed K and V vectors. Memory grows as <code>O(n · d · N · h)</code> per sequence, which becomes the primary memory bottleneck for long-context inference. Grouped Query Attention (sidenote 17) directly addresses this.</span></div>
			{/* Section 09: Circuits sidenotes */}
			<div class="sidenote" id="sn-circuits-olah"><span class="sn-number">28</span><span class="sn-content">Olah et al. “Zoom In: An Introduction to Circuits.” <em>Distill</em>, 2020. Proposes the circuits framework for understanding neural networks as compositions of interpretable features and weights. <a href="https://distill.pub/2020/circuits/zoom-in/" class="sn-link">link</a></span></div>
			<div class="sidenote" id="sn-induction"><span class="sn-number">29</span><span class="sn-content">The induction head circuit was also independently characterized in Elhage et al. (2021). It appears reliably in 2+ layer transformers and accounts for a substantial fraction of in-context learning performance.</span></div>
			<div class="sidenote" id="sn-mi-olah"><span class="sn-number">30</span><span class="sn-content">Wang et al. “Interpretability in the Wild: a Circuit for Indirect Object Identification in GPT-2 Small.” <em>ICLR</em> 2023. A detailed circuit analysis identifying 26 heads involved in the IOI task. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.00593" class="sn-link">arXiv</a></span></div>
			<div class="sidenote" id="sn-saes"><span class="sn-number">31</span><span class="sn-content">Sparse autoencoders decompose residual stream activations into interpretable features. Bricken et al. “Towards Monosemanticity,” Anthropic, 2023. Recent scaling work (Templeton et al., 2024) extracted millions of interpretable features from Claude. <a href="https://transformer-circuits.pub/2023/monosemantic-features/index.html" class="sn-link">link</a></span></div>
			{/* Head walkthrough annotation — positioned next to figure on desktop */}
			<div id="hw-gutter-ann" class="hw-gutter-ann">
				<div id="hw-gutter-ann-title" class="head-walk-ann-title">The Black Box</div>
				<div id="hw-gutter-ann-text" class="head-walk-ann-text">We will open this black box step by step, tracing an input sentence through every operation inside a single attention head.</div>
				<div id="hw-gutter-ann-shape" class="head-walk-ann-shape"></div>
			</div>
		</aside>
	</div>

